## Purpose

The current implementation has revealed a real interaction problem.

Every Tell remains visible for the same duration.

Watching the onboarding experience demonstrated that this feels unnatural.

A short welcome message lingers too long.

A long explanatory message requires more time.

The implementation should therefore be revised.

---

## Important architectural clarification

Do NOT think of this as "Journey waiting."

Journey still knows nothing about timing.

Journey still knows only one thing:

The current Step reports success.

The ownership is:

Journey
waits only for success.

TellStep
determines for itself when it has successfully completed its own presentation lifecycle.

A Tell therefore owns:

- fade in
- visible presentation
- visible hold duration
- fade out
- transparent pause
- reporting success

Journey must never know how long any of those phases lasted.

---

## Goal

Each Tell Step should define how long it remains fully visible.

TellStepView should continue to own:

- fade timing
- pause timing

The Tell definition should own only:

visible hold duration

The visible hold duration is therefore part of the Tell Step's own completion rule.

---

## Implementation

Revise the Tell definition to contain one additional Tell-specific field:

visible hold duration

Represent it in the simplest durable form suitable for the current schema.

Likely:

hold_duration_ms

Do not introduce:

- timing profiles
- presentation policy
- timing services
- calculated reading speed
- word-count heuristics
- animation configuration objects

The field exists only because the onboarding experiment demonstrated a real need.

---

## Tell lifecycle

Tell completion becomes:

fade in
↓

remain visible for this Tell's own hold duration
↓

fade out
↓

transparent pause
↓

report success

Journey advances only after Tell reports success.

